rized parallelism and distributed training, providing unified infrastructures and interfaces for quantum programming. It can compose quantum circuits, neural networks and tensor networks seamlessly with high simulation efficiency and flexibility.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:76:TensorCircuit-NG is built on top of modern machine learning frameworks: Jax, TensorFlow, and PyTorch. It is specifically suitable for large-scale simulations of quantum-classical hybrid paradigm and variational quantum algorithms in ideal (`Circuit`), noisy (`DMCircuit`), Clifford (`StabilizerCircuit`), qudit (`QuditCircuit`), approximate (`MPSCircuit`), analog (`AnalogCircuit`), symmetric (`U1Circuit`) and fermionic (`FGSCircuit`) cases. It also supports quantum hardware access and provides CPU/GPU/QPU hybrid deployment solutions.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:78:TensorCircuit-NG is the technical successor to TensorCircuit, led and maintained by the original TensorCircuit development team. This distribution has served as the primary home for the framework's evolution, addressing critical maintenance gaps (numpy > 2.0, qiskit > 1.0) and feature enhancements. As a fully compatible [drop-in replacement](https://tensorcircuit-ng.readthedocs.io/en/latest/faq.html#what-is-the-relation-between-tensorcircuit-and-tensorcircuit-ng), TensorCircuit-NG delivers next-gen capabilities—including stabilizer/qudit/analog/symmetric circuit simulation and multi-node multi-GPU distributed simulation.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:87:For beginners, please refer to [quantum computing lectures with TC-NG](https://github.com/sxzgroup/qc_lecture) to learn both quantum computing basics and representative usage of TensorCircuit-NG.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:123:- Circuit construction:\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:127:c = tc.Circuit(2)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:132:print(c.expectation_ps(z=[0, 1]))\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:139:tc.set_backend(\"jax\")\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:148:    c = tc.Circuit(2)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:150:    return tc.backend.real(c.expectation((tc.gates.z(), [0])))\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:152:g = tc.backend.grad(forward)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:153:g = tc.backend.jit(g)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:159:  <summary> More highlight features for TensorCircuit (click for details) </summary>\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:161:- Sparse Hamiltonian generation and expectation evaluation:\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:176:c = tc.Circuit(n)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:178:energy = tc.templates.measurements.operator_expectation(c, h)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:187:c = tc.Circuit(n)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:190:c.expectation_ps(z=[0, n-1], reuse=False)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:196:c = tc.DMCircuit(2)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:244:  - Space: [1000+ qubits 1D VQE workflow](https://re-ra.xyz/%E5%9F%BA%E4%BA%8E-TensorCircuit-NG-%E7%9A%84%E5%8D%83%E6%AF%94%E7%89%B9%E4%B8%87%E5%8F%82%E6%95%B0%E5%85%A8%E6%A2%AF%E5%BA%A6%E9%87%8F%E5%AD%90%E7%AE%97%E6%B3%95%E8%AE%AD%E7%BB%83/) (converged energy inaccuracy: < 0.5%)\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:247:  - Flexibility: customized contraction, multiple ML backend/interface choices, multiple dtype precisions, multiple QPU providers\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:260:  - Support **stabilizer circuit simulation** with stim backend.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:266:  - Support **Fermion Gaussian state** simulation with expectation, entanglement, measurement, ground state, real and imaginary time evolution.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:278:  - **Scalable readout error mitigation** native to both bitstring and expectation level with automatic qubit mapping consideration.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:282:  - Support **MPS/MPO** as representations for input states, quantum gates and observables to be measured.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:284:  - Support **vectorized parallelism** on circuit inputs, circuit parameters, circuit structures, circuit measurements and these vectorization can be nested.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:292:  - Support **symmetry-enforced circuit simulation** with `U1Circuit` for charge conservation.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:296:  - Circuit sampling supports both final state sampling and perfect sampling from tensor networks.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:298:  - Light cone reduction support for local expectation calculation.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:302:  - Observables are supported in measurement, sparse matrix, dense matrix and MPO format.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:306:  - Reusable common circuit/measurement/problem templates and patterns.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:326:This project is created and maintained by [Shi-Xin Zhang](https://github.com/refraction-ray) with current core authors [Shi-Xin Zhang](https://github.com/refraction-ray) and [Yu-Qin Chen](https://github.com/yutuer21) (see the [brief history](/HISTORY.md) of TensorCircuit and TensorCircuit-NG). We also thank [contributions](https://github.com/tensorcircuit/tensorcircuit-ng/graphs/contributors) from the open source community.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:330:If this project helps in your research, please cite the two versions of software whitepapers to acknowledge the work put into the development of TensorCircuit-NG.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:332:- [TensorCircuit: a Quantum Software Framework for the NISQ Era](https://quantum-journal.org/papers/q-2023-02-02-912/) (published in Quantum).\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:334:- [TensorCircuit-NG: A Universal, Composable, and Scalable Platform for Quantum Computing and Quantum Simulation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14167).\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:338:Research works citing TensorCircuit-NG can be highlighted in [Research and Applications section](https://github.com/tensorcircuit/tensorcircuit-ng#research-and-applications).\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:348:TensorCircuit-NG is open source, released under the Apache License, Version 2.0.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:427:TensorCircuit-NG is a powerful framework for driving research and applications in quantum computing. Below are examples of published academic works (150+ in total) and open-source projects that utilize TensorCircuit and TensorCircuit-NG.\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:461:[TenCirChem](https://github.com/tencent-quantum-lab/TenCirChem) is an efficient and versatile quantum computation package for molecular properties. TenCirChem is based on TensorCircuit and is optimized for chemistry applications. The latest version TenCirChem-NG is open source and available at [TenCirChem-NG](https://github.com/tensorcircuit/TenCirChem-NG).\n/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/tensorcircuit_nightly-1.8.0.dev20260726.dist-info/METADATA:485:For the simulation implementation of quantum states based on neural networks, tensor networs and quantum circuits using TensorCircuit-NG, see the [project repo](https://github.com/sxzgroup/et).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
